# The 1000-line ratchet — enforced by scripts/check-file-size.sh, which runs in the CI `gates`
# job, the pre-commit and pre-push hooks, and `bn verify` / `bn wt merge`'s pre-land gate.
#
# One entry per source file that was ALREADY over the limit when the ratchet landed, recorded as
# "<lines>\t<path>" and sorted by path. Everything below is grandfathered debt, not permission.
#
# The rules:
#   * a file NOT listed here may never exceed 1000 lines           -> OVERSIZE
#   * a file listed here may never gain a single line              -> GREW
#   * a file listed here that shrinks must be re-baselined, so the
#     slack it just freed can never be reclaimed                   -> SHRANK
#   * a file listed here that drops to <=1000 must be REMOVED      -> GRADUATED
#   * an entry for a file that no longer exists must be removed    -> STALE
#
# Lines are raw `wc -l` — the same number your editor, `git diff --stat` and `wc` already show.
# No comment stripping, deliberately: the never-grow rule means the only way to satisfy the gate
# on a file that needs to grow is to SPLIT it, never to compress it.
#
# Regenerate after a legitimate shrink, split or delete, then commit the diff — this file is the
# ledger of that work, and it should only ever get shorter:
#
#   scripts/check-file-size.sh --update
